---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DirectionChart from '../components/DirectionChart.astro';
import MechanismChart from '../components/MechanismChart.astro';
import Corridor from '../components/Corridor.astro';
import JikongCircle from '../components/JikongCircle.astro';
import Residents from '../components/Residents.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="The Argument" current="argument" description="Five bridges in five chapters: the documented history of India–Korea Buddhist exchange, 300–2026.">
  <style>
    .chapter { margin-top: 4rem; }
    .chip {
      display: inline-block; font: 600 0.7rem/1.4 var(--sans); letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--paper); padding: 0.15rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 4px;
      margin-right: 0.5rem;
    }
    .refs { font: 0.72rem/1.5 var(--sans); color: var(--faint); }
    .toc { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.88rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 0; list-style: none; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 1.4rem; }
    .toc a { text-decoration: none; }
  </style>

  <div class="wrap" style="padding-top: 3rem;">
    <p class="eyebrow">The argument in five chapters</p>
    <h1>Five bridges, one silence</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Follow the documented record from the Silla monks who walked to India to the
      institutions that rebuilt the connection in our own time. Every claim below cites the
      record IDs and sources behind it; every figure has its data table attached.
    </p>
    <ol class="toc">
      <li><a href="#ch1">1 · Pilgrims</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ch2">2 · The master from Magadha</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ch3">3 · The long silence</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ch4">4 · Paper bridges</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ch5">5 · The institutional turn</a></li>
      <li><a href="#residents">Coda · Four residents</a></li>
    </ol>

    <!-- ································ chapter 1 ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch1">
      <p><span class="chip" style="background: var(--b1); color: #2e2503;">B1 · 300–900</span></p>
      <h2>Pilgrims: when Korea went to India</h2>
      <p>
        The first bridge was made of journeys. Eighteen carried mediators belong to the era
        before 900 — all of them monastics: eleven pilgrims, six scholars, a translator. This is
        the only period in sixteen centuries when movement between the two ends of Asia was
        <strong>balanced</strong>: eight travelled toward India, eight carried Buddhism toward
        Korea, and Hyecho did both.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its best-documented life is Hyecho (혜초, 704–787), who sailed from China to India around
        719 and returned overland through Kashmir, Persia and Central Asia — leaving the
        <em>Wang ocheonchukguk jeon</em>, the only surviving eighth-century East Asian record of
        India and Central Asia. The manuscript spent a millennium in the Dunhuang cave library,
        was identified by Paul Pelliot in 1908, and was first shown in Korea only in December
        2010 <span class="refs">(P-0001 · T-0001 · TX-0001 · EV-0019 · S-0048, S-0039)</span>.
      </p>
      <Corridor />
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Reading the evidence.</strong> Most B1 lives survive only through Yijing's
        seventh-century memoir of monks who went west and through hagiographies compiled
        centuries later. Nine of the eighteen have no dated activity at all — they are drawn from
        floruits, and the timeline renders them as fuzzy spans, never as points. Several never
        returned: the record of this bridge is in large part a record of monks who died on it.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ································ chapter 2 ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch2">
      <p><span class="chip" style="background: var(--b2);">B2 · 900–1392</span></p>
      <h2>The master from Magadha</h2>
      <p>
        The second bridge is a single circle of people. Every carried B2 mediator is a Seon
        figure, and the era's connective center is <strong>Dhyānabhadra</strong> (지공, d. 1363), a
        monk from Magadha who reached Goryeo via Yuan China in 1326 — the era's only documented
        India-born arrival, and its only physical journey. Around him the data records exactly
        four teacher–disciple ties: Naong Hyegeun, Muhak Chach'o, Baegun Gyeonghan, and Ch'ukwŏn
        Chijŏn — men who would go on to shape late-Goryeo Seon and advise the first Joseon king
        <span class="refs">(P-0023 · R-0003, R-0019, R-0020, R-0022 · T-0013)</span>.
      </p>
      <JikongCircle />
      <p>
        The direction has already reversed. No Korean monk of this era is documented travelling
        to India; India, in one person, came to Korea — and his influence then travelled through
        lineage, not movement. When the dynasty fell in 1392, this circle was the whole living
        bridge; it did not survive the century.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ································ chapter 3 ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch3">
      <p><span class="chip" style="background: var(--void); color: var(--ink);">B3 · 1392–1900</span></p>
      <h2>The long silence</h2>
      <p>
        For the five hundred years of Joseon, the dataset carries <strong>zero records</strong> —
        and that number is the most carefully established fact in it. Eleven candidate rows were
        classified for this period and all eleven were excluded on evidence: the Buddhist state
        gave way to a Neo-Confucian one, monastics lost access to the capital, and no documented
        person, journey, text, or institution connects Korean Buddhism to India across the entire
        era under this project's inclusion criteria.
      </p>
      <p>
        The silence must be read precisely. It is a documented absence under stated criteria —
        not proof that no contact occurred, and not missing data. The one genuine research lead,
        Yi Kyu-gyŏng's nineteenth-century encyclopedia entries on India, remains an open question
        for a future collection round, and is recorded as such rather than promoted into a
        record. Between the enshrinement of Dhyānabhadra's relics in 1370 and the next documented
        event in 1981 lie <strong>611 years</strong> <span class="refs">(EV-0017 · EV-0015 ·
        coverage analysis §6)</span>.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Why show an empty chapter?</strong> Because the interruption is the argument.
        A continuous "Buddhist bridge" narrative has to skate over these five centuries; the
        documented record does not permit it. Transmission survived — but in the next chapter it
        travels by print and lineage, not by anyone's feet.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ································ chapter 4 ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch4">
      <p><span class="chip" style="background: var(--b4);">B4 · 1900–1945</span></p>
      <h2>Paper bridges</h2>
      <p>
        When the connection re-emerges under colonial rule, it has changed material. Of the six
        carried mediators of 1900–1945, four are not Buddhists at all, and almost nobody
        travels: the era's two attempted pilgrimages both end unrealized. The young monk
        Yi Yŏng-jae vowed the journey to India and died in 1927, at twenty-seven, without
        completing it; Rabindranath Tagore's planned 1937 visit to Korea never took place
        <span class="refs">(P-0072 · P-0030 · S-0075, S-0013)</span>.
      </p>
      <DirectionChart />
      <p>
        What crossed instead was text. Tagore is the era's hub: translated into Korean by Kim Ŏk,
        Chu Yo-han, Jeong Ji-yong and others, met in Tokyo by Ch'oe Nam-sŏn's circle, and author
        of the six-line 1929 message to the <em>Dong-a Ilbo</em> that became famous as "The Lamp
        of the East." The record here is exact: the six-line message is authentic; the fifteen-line
        poem widely reproduced under that title is a later composite, and the dataset documents
        the fabrication rather than repeating it <span class="refs">(P-0030 · R-0006–R-0010 ·
        TX-0007)</span>. Buddhism, when it appears, appears the same way — Han Yong-un reading
        Indian thought into <em>Buddhist reform</em>, not sailing for Bodh Gaya.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Who is missing.</strong> The translators who actually carried Tagore into Korean
        are outside the carried set — their connection is to a text, not to Buddhist India — and
        no woman appears anywhere in the carried record before 1990. Both absences are documented
        properties of the dataset, discussed in <a href={`${base}/data/`}>Data &amp; Method</a>.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ································ chapter 5 ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch5">
      <p>
        <span class="chip" style="background: var(--b5);">B5 · 1945–1990</span>
        <span class="chip" style="background: var(--b6);">B6 · 1990–2026</span>
      </p>
      <h2>The institutional turn</h2>
      <p>
        The postwar thread is thin — five mediators in forty-five years, led by the monk-poet
        Pŏpchŏng's engagement with Gandhi's thought and the archaeologist Hwang Su-yŏng's 1962
        journey to Nalanda <span class="refs">(P-0052 · T-0042)</span>. Then, from the 1990s, the
        bridge is rebuilt a fifth time — and for the first time its builders are
        <strong>institutions</strong>: the Jogye Order, Dongguk and Delhi universities, the
        International Buddhist Confederation, temple foundations at Bodh Gaya, MOUs, summits,
        relic returns, and organized mass pilgrimage.
      </p>
      <MechanismChart />
      <p>
        The record's nineteen events all fall after 1981, and they cluster hard in the last
        decade: the 2023 Sangwol walking pilgrimage — 108 Korean Buddhists, 1,167 km on foot
        from Sarnath toward Lumbini — the 2024 return of Hoeamsa relics from Boston, closing a
        circle opened in 1370, and a calendar of summits and pilgrimages running into 2027
        <span class="refs">(EV-0001 · EV-0018 · EV-0003, EV-0004, EV-0012)</span>. Movement is
        back — seven of the eight B6 mediators travelled — and so, for the first time since
        Hyecho, is bidirectionality. The era also admits the record's first women: the
        bhikkhunis Hyoseok and Wookwan, both trained in Indian universities
        <span class="refs">(P-0063 · P-0064)</span>.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>An honesty check before the conclusion.</strong> B6 is the record's
        best-documented era because it is the news era: 51 of 187 sources are press reports,
        and they concentrate here, while the whole ancient layer rests on a small core of
        primary and archival texts. The dataset therefore treats modern density as a property
        of documentation, not proof of greater historical weight — the
        <a href={`${base}/timeline/`}>timeline</a> keeps a source-density ribbon in view for
        exactly this reason.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ································ coda ·· -->
    <section class="chapter" id="residents">
      <p class="eyebrow">Coda</p>
      <h2>Sixteen centuries, four residents</h2>
      <p>
        One human role recurs in every era of contact and exists in no other: the mediator who
        stays. Marananta, who brought Buddhism to Baekje in 384; Baedalta, with whom Gyeomik
        translated the Vinaya; Dhyānabhadra, who died at Hoeamsa in 1363; and Ven. Buddhavara,
        resident at Bodh Gaya today. Four people in sixteen hundred years — and none at all in
        the five centuries of silence. If the five bridges have a single continuous thread,
        it is this one, and it is made of residence, not passage.
      </p>
      <Residents />
      <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <strong>The argument, restated.</strong> There was never one bridge. Each era built its
        own — from bodies, from lineage, from paper, from institutions — in its own direction,
        and each ended. What the documented record shows is not continuity but
        <em>reconstruction</em>: five generations that, mostly unaware of each other, kept
        deciding the crossing was worth building again.
      </p>
      <div class="doors">
        <a class="door" href={`${base}/timeline/`}>
          <p class="door-title">Explore the timeline</p>
          <p>All 182 dated marks, with uncertainty rendered honestly and the presets that answer
          this chapter's questions.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="door" href={`${base}/people/`}>
          <p class="door-title">Meet the 43 mediators</p>
          <p>The full prosopographical register — every person, every source.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
